 "drivingQuestion": "Why does the mishna call the certain sons 'unflawed', as though the mixed ones were unfit?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "resolved by Rav Pappa's emendation",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.99b.5",
   "daf": "99b",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מֵתוּ הַכְּשֵׁרִים וְכוּ׳. אֶלָּא הָנָךְ, מִשּׁוּם דְּאִיעָרוּב לְהוּ הָווּ לְהוּ פְּסוּלִין? אָמַר רַב פָּפָּא, אֵימָא: וּמֵתוּ הַוַּדָּאִין.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» It is stated in the mishna that if «the» sons of certain, «unflawed» lineage were the «ones who died,» the mixed sons perform ḥalitza with the widows of the elder woman’s sons, but not levirate marriage. The Gemara asks: Does this indicate that «because these» sons «were mixed up they are» rendered «unfit?» The fact that their lineage is unclear should not render them unfit. «Rava Pappa said: Say» that the correct wording is: «And if the certain» sons were the ones who «died.»"
  }
 ],
